direction: down

title: |md
  ## FIB: the Forwarding Information Base
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

flow: "Lookup input: flowi4" {
  style.fill: "#dbeafe"
  f_body: |md
    struct flowi4 holds the lookup key:
    - daddr (dest IP)
    - saddr (src IP, may be 0)
    - tos / DSCP
    - oif (output interface, may be 0)
    - iif (input interface, may be 0)
    - flowi4_mark (skb mark)

    The lookup happens against a fib_table.
  |
}

trie: "fib_trie: LC-trie data structure" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  t_body: |md
    Linux uses LC-trie (level-compressed trie)
    for the routing table.

    - Path-compressed: skip levels with single child
    - Branch factor adapts to data density
    - Lookup: O(W) where W = key bits (32 for IPv4)
    - In practice: 5-15 memory accesses per lookup

    See net/ipv4/fib_trie.c — search fib_table_lookup
  |
}

result: "Result: struct fib_result" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  r_body: |md
    Lookup returns:
    - prefixlen of the matching route
    - nhc (next hop, struct fib_nh_common)
    - type (UNICAST, LOCAL, BROADCAST, MULTICAST)
    - tclassid
    - table id

    Higher-level wrappers (ip_route_input,
    ip_route_output_flow) take this and construct
    a struct dst_entry / rtable for the skb.
  |
}

flow -> trie -> result
